 "meta": {
  "title": "The devil played for him in a dream. He never got over it.",
  "description": "Giuseppe Tartini (1692–1770) told the astronomer Jérôme Lalande that his Violin Sonata in G minor, the \"Devil's Trill\", came from a dream in which the devil played his violin. Lalande printed the account in his Voyage d'un François en Italie (1769). The picture is Le Songe de Tartini, a lithograph by Louis-Léopold Boilly from 1824.",
  "tags": [
   "art history",
   "classical music",
   "Tartini",
   "Devil's Trill"
  ],
  "question": "Begging the devil to stop, or begging him to play on?"
 },
 "sources": [
  {
   "claim": "Tartini's account of the dream (the devil as his servant, the violin handed over, the sonata, waking breathless, the piece 'so inferior' to what he heard, he would have broken his violin and given up music if he could have lived without it), recorded by Jérôme Lalande",
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Violin_Sonata_in_G_minor_(Tartini)",
   "note": "quoting Lalande, Voyage d'un François en Italie (1769)"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Giuseppe Tartini, violinist and composer, 1692–1770; the Devil's Trill is his best-known work",
